<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The desktop hub speaks the frozen wire contract documented in TransferClient: plain HTTP on
    port 8756, one request at a time, no TLS option. From targetSdk 28 onward Android denies
    cleartext by default, so without this file every transfer dies at the socket with
    UnknownServiceException - an IOException, which the client reports as "Could not reach the
    desktop". That message sends an operator off to check the Wi-Fi for a fault that is not on
    the Wi-Fi at all.

    Why this is a base-config rather than a scoped list of the private ranges:
    <domain> takes hostnames and IP literals but does NOT understand CIDR notation, so
    "192.168.0.0/16" silently never matches and a desktop at 192.168.50.55 is still blocked. The
    operator types the desktop's address by hand and it can be anywhere on the warehouse network,
    so there is no finite list of hosts to enumerate ahead of time.

    The exposure is bounded and worth stating plainly: this app makes no other network calls, and
    the hub traffic is plaintext only to a desktop on the operator's own LAN, carrying an
    inventory document and no credentials.

    If the hub ever gains HTTPS, delete this file and use the URL the desktop reports. TLS on the
    desktop is the right fix; a wider exemption here is the expedient one.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>